Dean?

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:07.916
DEAN HILDEBRAND: Thanks, Dave.

00:05:07.916 --> 00:05:08.820
All right.

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:12.690
So I wanted to just give a
brief intro on a few things.

00:05:12.690 --> 00:05:16.590
One, just talking briefly
about file in the Cloud,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.390
and as well as, why does Google
care about file in the Cloud?

00:05:21.390 --> 00:05:24.680
All right, so here's
the typical scenario.

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:26.521
A company is going to
move into the Cloud.

00:05:26.521 --> 00:05:27.020
All right.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:28.790
This sounds awesome.

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:32.970
Google has a bunch of
application migration tools.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:33.470
All right.

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:36.110
Let's move the applications
into the Cloud.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:39.920
Next step is, well, what
do we do with the storage?

00:05:39.920 --> 00:05:41.900
Are we going to
start from scratch?

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.630
Are we going to
rethink everything

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:46.210
we ever thought about storage?

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.760
Does the Cloud solve
all of our problems?

00:05:48.760 --> 00:05:49.820
What are we going to do?

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:54.560
So I think that this becomes a
general blocker in many cases

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:57.140
when you have
terabytes to petabytes

00:05:57.140 --> 00:06:00.030
or more data on premise,
and you're just not sure,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:02.030
not only where to store
it, but how to store it,

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:05.980
and how your applications
will access it.

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:10.150
So I think some of the
no-nos generally are--

00:06:10.150 --> 00:06:12.310
there is no one thing.

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:15.060
So object storage,
GCS, it's awesome,

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:17.410
and I'm going to talk
briefly about it.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.090
It's not a requirement
by any means.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.190
You can't say, oh, I
can't move to Cloud

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:24.310
because my workload doesn't use
object storage, or something

00:06:24.310 --> 00:06:25.730
to that effect.

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:27.250
It doesn't work that way, right?

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:29.470
We want to make
sure the matching is

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:30.554
great to your application.

00:06:30.554 --> 00:06:32.594
The other one is, we don't
want to look at, well,

00:06:32.594 --> 00:06:34.540
look at those customers
that are on stage.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:36.940
They used this storage solution.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:38.610
That's the one I have to use.

00:06:38.610 --> 00:06:40.180
Well, I mean, you could
have completely different

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:41.020
applications, right?

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:44.530
So really I think we need
to ignore all that hype,

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:48.610
and then think about more just,
what do your applications need?

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:51.190
Just focus on the applications
that you're running.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:54.100
How are they running today on
what the existing systems are,

00:06:54.100 --> 00:06:56.620
and then what do they
need in the Cloud?

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:00.817
So what can we modernize,
and where does innovation

00:07:00.817 --> 00:07:01.900
and new things make sense?

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:05.520
And where does just using
what you're doing today

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:07.340
to make a lot of sense?

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:08.050
All right.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:10.990
So GCS-- GCS is awesome.

00:07:10.990 --> 00:07:12.970
People always ask,
well, why aren't you

00:07:12.970 --> 00:07:14.620
pushing people into GCS?

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.200
And the point is is that,
well, many applications just

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:18.410
don't fit the model.

00:07:18.410 --> 00:07:20.990
And so the POSIX
API is one of them.

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:25.840
But even if you put the
POSIX API on top of GCS,

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.820
there's still a million
other requirements

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:31.450
where object storage as a
concept in which it's been

00:07:31.450 --> 00:07:33.380
implemented just doesn't fit.

00:07:33.380 --> 00:07:37.770
It has long latency times,
no in-place updates,

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.400
small random reads.

00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.850
It can be really tricky in
terms of how you're doing them.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:46.420
And you can always copy them
into the individual nodes

00:07:46.420 --> 00:07:48.490
and process them there,
but if your dataset

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:51.665
is larger than what you
can process in a single VM,

00:07:51.665 --> 00:07:53.290
you run yourself into
trouble, as well.

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:58.330
So let's go back to think
about NFS again, and see

00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.760
how that can fit.

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:03.850
But I talk to some people
that are like, well,

00:08:03.850 --> 00:08:08.162
NFS, I've been using
NFS, and it's slow.

00:08:08.162 --> 00:08:10.120
Why are you telling me
to use NFS in the Cloud?

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.175
I'm very confused on this thing.

00:08:12.175 --> 00:08:15.830
Well, the point is,
is that NFS is modern.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.650
It is Cloud-friendly, and
it does work really well.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:21.160
There's no reason to dump
the baby with the bathwater

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:22.990
as they say.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:26.500
NFS is built into all
of the VMs you deploy.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.420
It works very well
with Kubernetes.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:31.232
It's preemptible, very
friendly in the sense

00:08:31.232 --> 00:08:32.440
that, just take your VM away.

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:32.939
Right?

00:08:32.939 --> 00:08:35.049
NFS already stored
your data somewhere.

00:08:35.049 --> 00:08:37.287
And in many ways
it's very scalable.

00:08:37.287 --> 00:08:39.370
It can support tens of
thousands of cores, as well

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:42.650
as it's familiar to your
IT administration staff.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.710
And so that's something
where we really

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:48.290
think we have something
that we can build upon.

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:52.330
The problem, though, is today,
NFS in the Cloud I would argue

00:08:52.330 --> 00:08:54.550
is in a very immature place.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:57.220
And we're really just at
the beginning of the journey

00:08:57.220 --> 00:09:00.970
to start thinking about,
well, maybe we don't need

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:02.422
to do it exactly the same way.

00:09:02.422 --> 00:09:03.880
And maybe we need
to think about it

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:09.160
in a slightly different
manner than when we

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:10.570
were deploying it on-premise.

00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:13.000
And so one of the things
is, open source storage

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:14.140
can be tricky.

00:09:14.140 --> 00:09:16.240
Talk about yak
shaving-- there can

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.730
be a lot of yak shaving
in unmanaged open source

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:20.680
solutions.

00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:22.810
The vendor ecosystem
is growing, and we're

00:09:22.810 --> 00:09:25.600
doing our best to improve
it, but it's still

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:26.750
in its early stages.

00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:28.570
And that's where
something becomes hard,

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:30.070
because a lot of
times you're having

00:09:30.070 --> 00:09:34.420
to take a new chance on a
new vendor inside the Cloud.

00:09:34.420 --> 00:09:39.580
And in a lot of cases, the
current NFS and file storage

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:41.366
options that you
can deploy, they

00:09:41.366 --> 00:09:42.490
were designed for hardware.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.730
And people are just doing
lift-and-shifts into the Cloud,

00:09:45.730 --> 00:09:48.790
and they're not handling new
failure models, performance

00:09:48.790 --> 00:09:51.860
characteristics, and how
the durability is managed.

00:09:51.860 --> 00:09:54.040
And so it's really
something where

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:56.170
we want to rethink
about, let's use NFS,

00:09:56.170 --> 00:09:58.870
but let's rethink about
how we're doing it.

00:09:58.870 --> 00:10:03.190
So what we want is file
storage, manage and scale it

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:06.640
just like the Cloud does, but
drive it like an enterprise.

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:09.520
We want to make sure that
this is enterprise-ready.

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:11.560
So back to the point
of, why does Google

00:10:11.560 --> 00:10:14.230
care, though, about this
stuff, this NFS stuff?

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:17.860
Well, you know, let's
look at EDA industry.

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:20.170
The industry was
at $8.5 billion.

00:10:20.170 --> 00:10:23.350
It's growing over the next
six years to $14 billion.

00:10:23.350 --> 00:10:26.390
And that's just the
software ecosystem itself.

00:10:26.390 --> 00:10:29.980
And so that's not including
any of the IT infrastructure

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:31.810
underneath the covers
that's supporting it.

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.960
And so Google wants to
be that IT infrastructure

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:37.180
that is supporting this
multibillion dollar

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:39.130
industry under the
covers, and why we're

00:10:39.130 --> 00:10:42.250
working with people like Naidu.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.070
But that's just one
of many industries

00:10:45.070 --> 00:10:47.710
where we're seeing
file as dominant,

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.859
and the software vendors would
have to completely rewrite

00:10:50.859 --> 00:10:52.150
how they're doing things today.

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:53.899
And it's not even clear
that it's possible

00:10:53.899 --> 00:10:55.550
even if they wanted to.

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:59.950
And so manufacturing, oil and
gas, higher education research,

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:02.500
financial services,
those quants that

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:06.480
are out there, health care,
genomics, and media, of course,

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.790
that you'll see a lot
here, as well-- and so all

00:11:08.790 --> 00:11:11.770
of these industries are
built on file today,

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:13.320
and it's something
where we believe

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.120
that if we can make
file really easy

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.650
and well integrated
into the Cloud,

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:19.260
then this is something that
Google Cloud can really

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:20.610
leverage.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:24.570
And so our mission
here at Google Cloud

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:28.800
is to make file storage awesome
and easy to use and just

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.260
as super cloud-integrated
as you would expect.

00:11:31.260 --> 00:11:33.900
And that's why we're so excited
to work with people like Naidu

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:37.440
at eSilicon who's really
pioneering how EDA

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.420
can be done inside the cloud.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:40.950
And that's what I have.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.310
Back to you, Dave.

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:43.300
Thank you very much.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:43.680
DAVE PAYNE: Thanks, Dean.

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:44.320
Let's hear it.

00:11:47.444 --> 00:11:48.360
I'll take the clicker.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:55.426
So let's-- what I wanted to do
then is show what this actually

00:11:55.426 --> 00:11:57.800
looks like, and get out of
the slides a bit and show some

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.450
action.

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:01.700
And as brave as I am
about doing live demos--

00:12:01.700 --> 00:12:03.110
it's one of my favorite things--

00:12:03.110 --> 00:12:05.277
I recorded these just for
you so we can get straight

00:12:05.277 --> 00:12:06.860
to the point and
cover a lot of topics

00:12:06.860 --> 00:12:08.970
in a short amount of time.

00:12:08.970 --> 00:12:13.490
So real briefly, let me tell you
a little bit about Elastifile.

00:12:13.490 --> 00:12:15.350
We're kind of a new
company into the scene.

00:12:15.350 --> 00:12:17.370
It came out of
stealth-mode last year.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:22.550
We built a full stack storage
product as an NFS filer,

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:25.940
designed to work with
cloud principles,

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:28.460
but still show up like
you're used to in enterprise.

00:12:28.460 --> 00:12:31.370
The idea that Dean was
talking about where

00:12:31.370 --> 00:12:34.100
you're comfortable living
in your enterprise home.

00:12:34.100 --> 00:12:36.590
Your application is built
there and it exists,

00:12:36.590 --> 00:12:39.350
and the underlying
infrastructure is set.

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:41.330
We designed ours to be
compatible with that,

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:43.160
but also move
straight into cloud.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.210
And I'll show you what
that looks like as we go.

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:47.730
So some simple things
that we allow you to do--

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:50.180
one is just take high
performance NFS applications

00:12:50.180 --> 00:12:51.900
and run them as-is in Cloud.

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.620
All of these semantics work
the way that you would expect.

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:56.360
I'll show some of that in a bit.

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:58.220
But we have it show
up inside of Google

00:12:58.220 --> 00:13:01.670
so your applications can just
lift, shift, and start running.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:04.850
Second is, we have some
capabilities to take advantage

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:07.310
of the different types of
storage inside of Google

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.650
without forcing you
to deal with it.

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:11.690
So there's storage
inside of Compute Engine,

00:13:11.690 --> 00:13:14.330
there's storage inside
of Cloud Storage.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:16.760
And we have the ability to
move data back and forth

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:19.590
and tier them without
breaking POSIX semantics

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.740
and the single
namespace and things

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.030
like that that matter
to your applications.

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:26.160
And then lastly, the
data mobility side of it,

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:27.920
we have a capability
called CloudConnect

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:31.340
that moves full datasets.

00:13:31.340 --> 00:13:33.950
We take snapshots of data
and ship them as a whole

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.829
back and forth so you can keep
your data integrity intact,

00:13:36.829 --> 00:13:38.870
but have it show up and
use it where you want it.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.330
So think about giving
lift to datasets

00:13:41.330 --> 00:13:44.180
that tend to have a
lot of gravity in them

00:13:44.180 --> 00:13:46.292
due to the nature of data.

00:13:46.292 --> 00:13:48.500
So these are the two components
I'll be talking about

00:13:48.500 --> 00:13:49.830
back and forth.

00:13:49.830 --> 00:13:53.190
On the far side is
the file system.

00:13:53.190 --> 00:13:56.360
This is a full-stack,
scale-out file system,

00:13:56.360 --> 00:13:58.010
so it presents
like an NFS server,

00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:59.660
but it's fully distributed.

00:13:59.660 --> 00:14:02.720
It's a shared-nothing,
write-anywhere architecture.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:06.620
So as we need to scale
capacity and performance,

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:09.850
the scaling unit is a
node, a little server.

00:14:09.850 --> 00:14:14.000
And that they just add
dynamically, laterally,

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:14.840
as you need more.

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:18.760
And as you don't need it as
much, it constricts back in.

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:19.950
So it's very elastic.

00:14:19.950 --> 00:14:23.450
And it actually works to
add and take away on demand.

00:14:23.450 --> 00:14:25.970
And then in Cloud,
what's magic about Cloud

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:29.490
is, you can instantiate
anything at the drop of a hat.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.480
So we can stand up
an Elastifile cluster

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:34.640
in mere minutes, the kind of
thing you wouldn't do on prem.

00:14:34.640 --> 00:14:39.770
You wouldn't stand up a filer
on a whim, on an afternoon

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:41.420
do a project and
then throw it away.

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:44.300
That's typically a
five-year process, right,

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:46.580
that we can shrink down
into a couple of minutes.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:49.610
And then lastly, CloudConnect
is a companion technology

00:14:49.610 --> 00:14:50.882
as part of our stack.

00:14:50.882 --> 00:14:53.090
And this is about reaching
out to datasets that don't

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.010
live inside of Elastifile yet.

00:14:55.010 --> 00:14:59.060
So CloudConnect can connect to
an existing filer or whatever

00:14:59.060 --> 00:15:02.370
that might be, grab the data,
take a complete snapshot of it

00:15:02.370 --> 00:15:04.470
that's identical
to what you have,

00:15:04.470 --> 00:15:08.789
all of the POSIX data,
anything about the namespace--

00:15:08.789 --> 00:15:10.205
attributes,
hardlinks, softlinks--

00:15:10.205 --> 00:15:13.120
it keeps all that intact,
compresses it, dedupes it

00:15:13.120 --> 00:15:16.430
and moves it into Cloud Storage,
and makes it ready for action

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:17.660
as a file system later.

00:15:17.660 --> 00:15:19.370
OK?

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:22.350
So that's what CloudConnect is.

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:25.970
I didn't take time to add
a demo of CloudConnect

00:15:25.970 --> 00:15:26.840
into this session.

00:15:26.840 --> 00:15:29.240
We did one yesterday
that was fantastic

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.930
that I'll show you a link to.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.512
And I encourage
you go on YouTube,

00:15:33.512 --> 00:15:34.970
watch some videos
about it, and you

00:15:34.970 --> 00:15:37.640
can contact us to download
and use this tool.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.440
But essentially it's
a connection point

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:42.530
between file infrastructure
and object infrastructures.

00:15:42.530 --> 00:15:44.480
You know, objects are
designed to store files

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.080
in a flat namespace.

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:49.160
So to create hierarchy and
logic in an object system,

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:51.500
you have to do that
yourself essentially.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.730
Whereas a file system shows
up and it's ready to go.

00:15:53.730 --> 00:15:55.313
I can create folders
and files, and it

00:15:55.313 --> 00:15:56.810
all kind of self-organizes.

00:15:56.810 --> 00:15:59.450
So CloudConnect bridges the
gap between those worlds.

00:15:59.450 --> 00:16:01.550
Instead of using
objects as files,

00:16:01.550 --> 00:16:03.930
each object is
essentially like a block.

00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:05.600
And then we can
store chunks of files

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:08.060
across millions or
billions of objects,

00:16:08.060 --> 00:16:10.010
and still maintain
a namespace that

00:16:10.010 --> 00:16:13.220
allows us to take many
versions of a file system,

00:16:13.220 --> 00:16:15.560
as many as you
like, and store it

00:16:15.560 --> 00:16:18.650
in the smallest
compacted space possible.

00:16:18.650 --> 00:16:21.589
So we can do lots of versions
of snapshots, compression

00:16:21.589 --> 00:16:23.630
and dedupe, and it's a
really space-efficient and

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:26.610
cost-effective way to store data
that you're not really using.

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.060
And it's also the basis for
the tiering capabilities

00:16:29.060 --> 00:16:30.650
that we have in the product.

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:32.270
OK?

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.130
So take a look at that.

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:36.530
There's a quick sneak
peek at the interface,

00:16:36.530 --> 00:16:39.190
of different object
providers that it supports.

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:42.530
It's really designed to go
from any source to any target

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:46.550
and be a middleman
in between them, OK?

00:16:46.550 --> 00:16:47.270
So take a look.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:49.850
This session happened
yesterday with Jerome.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:53.017
You can screen cap that
link and go watch it.

00:16:53.017 --> 00:16:55.100
And there's a bunch of
videos on YouTube, as well.

00:16:55.100 --> 00:16:55.850
OK?

00:16:55.850 --> 00:16:57.031
So let's move on.

00:16:57.031 --> 00:16:57.530
All right.

00:16:57.530 --> 00:17:00.260
So inside of Google
Cloud there's

00:17:00.260 --> 00:17:03.410
a couple of options for NFS.

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.230
There's a few, and
they're growing.

00:17:05.230 --> 00:17:08.750
And really briefly, being
announced just a few weeks ago

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:09.972
is Filestore.

00:17:09.972 --> 00:17:12.180
So I'm not going to spend
a lot of time on Filestore,

00:17:12.180 --> 00:17:15.410
but it's a fully
managed file system.

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:18.680
You can get a file system
mount, pay as you go,

00:17:18.680 --> 00:17:20.859
and start using it.

00:17:20.859 --> 00:17:24.020
It has fixed boundaries in
terms of how big it gets,

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:25.970
but it seems like a
really great product.

00:17:25.970 --> 00:17:28.069
On our side, where
Elastifile really fits

00:17:28.069 --> 00:17:33.010
is for applications that need
the kind of really demanding

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:34.850
problems to solve,
whether it's demanding

00:17:34.850 --> 00:17:37.130
in terms of capacity
or performance

00:17:37.130 --> 00:17:40.310
or both, and some of
that dynamic nature

00:17:40.310 --> 00:17:42.050
of those applications.

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:43.940
So Elastifile is
designed to grow

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:47.390
with you to whatever the
boundaries of your application

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:48.410
may be.

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:50.297
Unlimited is the
term that we use

00:17:50.297 --> 00:17:52.380
and that we think about
as we design and architect

00:17:52.380 --> 00:17:53.200
Elastifile.

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:53.960
OK?

00:17:53.960 --> 00:17:56.334
And it's been great to work
closely with the Google team,

00:17:56.334 --> 00:17:58.436
and take advantage
of some of the power

00:17:58.436 --> 00:17:59.810
of that infrastructure
underneath

00:17:59.810 --> 00:18:02.750
to really stretch what we
can do with this system.

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:05.870
So a little bit more
about ECFS, as we call it,

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.390
Elastifile Cloud File System.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.197
First of all, like I
said, it's familiar.

00:18:10.197 --> 00:18:12.030
You're going to find
an NFS interface that's

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:13.572
POSIX-compatible.

00:18:13.572 --> 00:18:15.530
Other protocols we'll
probably introduced later

00:18:15.530 --> 00:18:17.510
to make it even easier for you.

00:18:17.510 --> 00:18:20.150
Second, it's elastic,
truly, scale out,

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.610
scale in as your needs demand.

00:18:22.610 --> 00:18:25.340
There's a full API behind the
product, so every function

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:28.940
that we do, you can automate
it behind your automation

00:18:28.940 --> 00:18:30.980
tool of choice,
whatever that may be.

00:18:30.980 --> 00:18:33.110
It's fast, so we're
able to take advantage

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:35.720
of whatever underlying
infrastructure we attach to.

00:18:35.720 --> 00:18:39.074
So we attach to raw disks
inside of our nodes,

00:18:39.074 --> 00:18:40.990
and we can take advantage
of persistent disks,

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:44.542
persistent SSD, local SSD,
and even Cloud Storage,

00:18:44.542 --> 00:18:46.250
and then take advantage
of those and make

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:50.560
them available as a single
pool of usable capacity.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:54.110
And so that choice allows us
to get really cost-effective.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.630
You can match the
storage infrastructure

00:18:56.630 --> 00:18:58.987
to the cost nature of
whatever your problem is,

00:18:58.987 --> 00:19:01.070
and get the right amount
of performance out of it,

00:19:01.070 --> 00:19:02.050
and change your mind.

00:19:02.050 --> 00:19:04.550
It's like, again, instantiate
quickly and tear it back down.

00:19:04.550 --> 00:19:06.050
You can deploy 18
clusters a day.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:09.172
It's all good.

00:19:09.172 --> 00:19:11.630
It's flexible, so this stuff
can show up where you need it.

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:14.090
You know, Google is always
offering more and more places

00:19:14.090 --> 00:19:16.080
to compute all around the world.

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:17.810
We can follow them around.

00:19:17.810 --> 00:19:19.370
And then lastly,
the last two points

00:19:19.370 --> 00:19:22.070
I wanted to drive home before
we jump into the demos, a.,

00:19:22.070 --> 00:19:24.350
it's Cloud native,
meaning this was designed

00:19:24.350 --> 00:19:27.860
with the semantics of
Cloud in mind as opposed

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:29.646
to that of on-prem.

00:19:29.646 --> 00:19:31.020
And the world is
a lot different.

00:19:31.020 --> 00:19:33.019
You don't see the
infrastructure in the same way

00:19:33.019 --> 00:19:34.240
when you're running in Cloud.

00:19:34.240 --> 00:19:35.930
The interrupts are different.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.189
The failures look different.

00:19:37.189 --> 00:19:39.230
They're not hardware
failures we're dealing with.

00:19:39.230 --> 00:19:42.110
They're more logical
failures in the stack.

00:19:42.110 --> 00:19:44.630
And essentially you
designed expecting failures

00:19:44.630 --> 00:19:46.346
to occur all the time.

00:19:46.346 --> 00:19:47.720
So there are
different principles

00:19:47.720 --> 00:19:49.550
that you have to
bring to the table.

00:19:49.550 --> 00:19:51.250
We've designed for that.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:54.380
And then lastly, Dean
mentioned open source

00:19:54.380 --> 00:19:56.090
versus supported products.

00:19:56.090 --> 00:19:58.770
We're a full company
supporting these products

00:19:58.770 --> 00:20:01.640
with 224/7 support,
continual updates.

00:20:01.640 --> 00:20:03.680
When you're bringing
enterprise into Cloud,

00:20:03.680 --> 00:20:06.770
you need that sort of comfort
to be wrapped around it

00:20:06.770 --> 00:20:09.710
so everything just works,
and you can meet your SLA

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:12.230
as we meet our SLA to you.

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:13.290
OK?

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:13.790
All right.

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:15.440
So getting started, I'm
going to show this working.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:16.640
If you want to start--

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:19.640
in fact, if you're bored of my
presentation, follow this link

00:20:19.640 --> 00:20:21.080
or go into Cloud Console.

00:20:21.080 --> 00:20:22.730
Look in the marketplace.

00:20:22.730 --> 00:20:24.020
Search for Elastifile.

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:26.870
If you can't remember how to
spell that, search for NFS.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:30.950
And you'll see two options we
have up in the marketplace now.

00:20:30.950 --> 00:20:32.390
We have our file system.

00:20:32.390 --> 00:20:33.260
Click Deploy.

00:20:33.260 --> 00:20:35.819
As long as you have a
project, you can go.

00:20:35.819 --> 00:20:36.860
You'll be up and running.

00:20:36.860 --> 00:20:38.526
Answer a couple of
questions, and you'll

00:20:38.526 --> 00:20:40.110
be able to store
data in minutes.

00:20:40.110 --> 00:20:41.568
And then below
that, we're excited,

00:20:41.568 --> 00:20:45.200
we just released this last week,
is our storage provisioner.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.570
This is our connection
into Kubernetes.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:49.730
And so if you have a
Kubernetes, a GKE cluster

00:20:49.730 --> 00:20:52.700
running in Cloud, click,
button, button, button,

00:20:52.700 --> 00:20:54.770
and you're going to be
deploying NFS storage

00:20:54.770 --> 00:20:57.422
to store stateful data
from your containers.

00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:59.130
And I'll show you a
bit of that, as well.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:00.980
In fact, Jerome's
session I mentioned

00:21:00.980 --> 00:21:03.991
goes into a deep dive
on that topic yesterday.

00:21:03.991 --> 00:21:04.490
OK?

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:06.406
But once you're deployed,
you hit our console,

00:21:06.406 --> 00:21:07.970
it runs right out
of Google Cloud.

00:21:07.970 --> 00:21:09.440
And then any
application you have

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:11.870
that needs this can
just start working.

00:21:11.870 --> 00:21:13.180
All right?

00:21:13.180 --> 00:21:15.470
Let's show this stuff in action.

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:18.400
So demo topology I'm working
with today on the top

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.770
is a project inside
a CloudPlatform.

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:22.630
I have our file system deployed.

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:26.020
I've got a few standard Linux
clients running on CentOS.

00:21:26.020 --> 00:21:29.380
I have a Google Kubernetes
engine cluster running.

00:21:29.380 --> 00:21:31.322
And that's already deployed.

00:21:31.322 --> 00:21:33.280
And then I'll be running
different applications

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:34.660
from there.

00:21:34.660 --> 00:21:36.554
CloudConnect
connects the object.

00:21:36.554 --> 00:21:38.470
And then lastly, I have
an on-prem data center

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:41.470
that's connected through a VPN
tunnel to my private project,

00:21:41.470 --> 00:21:42.680
as well.

00:21:42.680 --> 00:21:45.040
All right, so storage--

00:21:45.040 --> 00:21:45.640
performance.

00:21:45.640 --> 00:21:47.110
Everyone talks
about performance.

00:21:47.110 --> 00:21:49.600
I did a couple of
performance demos for you

00:21:49.600 --> 00:21:51.100
just to get that off the table.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:53.590
We claim to be
high-performance storage,

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:55.959
and able to meet the needs
essentially where you are.

00:21:55.959 --> 00:21:58.000
So let me show you a couple
of different profiles

00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.010
I thought might be
interesting, and then I'll

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:02.200
shift into some of the
functionality of how

00:22:02.200 --> 00:22:04.000
this stuff works out there, OK?

00:22:04.000 --> 00:22:08.350
So three workloads--
one's a transactional

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:10.030
IO is one type of a workload.

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:12.650
You might think databases,
or lots of small files, lots

00:22:12.650 --> 00:22:14.680
of changes and the like.

00:22:14.680 --> 00:22:18.250
Elastifile was designed to be
a transactional file system.

00:22:18.250 --> 00:22:21.040
We excel in this area, where
a lot of other file systems

00:22:21.040 --> 00:22:23.290
you would think, this
isn't what you use NFS for.

00:22:23.290 --> 00:22:26.320
I wouldn't put an
Oracle database on NFS,

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:28.450
or MySQL or something.

00:22:28.450 --> 00:22:29.130
Here you can.

00:22:29.130 --> 00:22:32.320
So the demo I'm going
to show you starts here.

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:36.240
So I'm running a Kubernetes
environment on the top.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.560
You'll see I'm going
to start adding

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:40.900
more and more containers.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:43.180
And everyone it adds--
it's going quickly.

00:22:43.180 --> 00:22:45.940
I'll go back so you can
see that a little faster.

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:49.880
And then-- there we go.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:52.850
So the demos will go quick.

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:55.240
So as I add containers
and scale them,

00:22:55.240 --> 00:22:57.670
you'll see
performance down here.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:23:00.130
You've got throughput
measured in megabytes

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:01.390
to gigabytes per second.

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:03.880
You've got IOs, that's how
many transactions are happening

00:23:03.880 --> 00:23:06.420
per second in
thousands, that's the K.

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.010
And then latency is
along the bottom.

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:10.600
And then up on the
right in the corner

00:23:10.600 --> 00:23:13.990
you see how many connections
are to the file system.

00:23:13.990 --> 00:23:17.260
That's how many NFS connections
are being instantiated.

00:23:17.260 --> 00:23:19.060
So our objective with
this is to show you

00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:23.350
that we can maintain a linear
performance profile as load

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:24.717
is added to this system.

00:23:24.717 --> 00:23:25.800
So watch it one more time.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:26.300
Here we go.

00:23:29.381 --> 00:23:31.010
You see throughput going up.

00:23:31.010 --> 00:23:32.990
You see-- this is really
an IO-driven workload.

00:23:32.990 --> 00:23:37.740
So that hit about 300
or so thousand IOs.

00:23:37.740 --> 00:23:38.240
All right.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:39.770
Let's go to the next one.

00:23:39.770 --> 00:23:42.490
Second profile-- large
file random read.

00:23:42.490 --> 00:23:43.820
So you have big bulk files.

00:23:43.820 --> 00:23:45.900
This might be media and
entertainment or genomics

00:23:45.900 --> 00:23:48.710
or something, where you have
these massive media or image

00:23:48.710 --> 00:23:50.720
files that need to
be loaded up quickly

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.240
for processing, whether
that's for AI or ML

00:23:53.240 --> 00:23:55.129
or something else like that.

00:23:55.129 --> 00:23:56.420
We'll show you how we do there.

00:24:02.702 --> 00:24:03.910
So watch the throughput here.

00:24:03.910 --> 00:24:05.743
We're trying to get to
gigabytes per second.

00:24:11.570 --> 00:24:14.490
I'm time lapsing all of this to
not bore you with the details,

00:24:14.490 --> 00:24:17.270
but this is about 10
minutes worth of loading.

00:24:17.270 --> 00:24:20.449
And it hits about 5.2 gigabytes
per second in the end.

00:24:20.449 --> 00:24:22.990
So this is a small cluster, this
kind of entry-level cluster.

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:25.070
If we need more, we
just keep adding more.

00:24:25.070 --> 00:24:26.236
What do you want to get to--

00:24:26.236 --> 00:24:28.110
10, 20, 50 gigabytes per second?

00:24:28.110 --> 00:24:29.360
And we can just keep on going.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:30.320
OK?

00:24:30.320 --> 00:24:32.660
You'll see we only had 24
connections that we're already

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:34.510
pushing that much IO.

00:24:34.510 --> 00:24:37.310
We're hitting bottlenecks
inside of the Kubernetes engine,

00:24:37.310 --> 00:24:39.518
actually, where we need to
keep adding infrastructure

00:24:39.518 --> 00:24:41.990
to introduce more
load to this system.

00:24:41.990 --> 00:24:44.270
Lastly, the opposite,
large file writes.

00:24:44.270 --> 00:24:46.969
So maybe I'm generating large
files, images and the like.

00:24:46.969 --> 00:24:49.010
I need to push those
quickly down into my storage

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:50.240
persistence layer.

00:24:50.240 --> 00:24:52.100
How fast can I do that?

00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:55.100
So same infrastructure here.

00:24:55.100 --> 00:24:57.560
We'll let this guy spin up.

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:00.500
You watch throughput again.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:02.000
So as we're adding
more nodes, we're

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.510
pushing up to about a 10-gig
connection right about now.

00:25:08.260 --> 00:25:10.290
So we found-- we're at 1.1.

00:25:10.290 --> 00:25:13.574
So that ends up at
about 1.2 or so,

00:25:13.574 --> 00:25:15.490
which is essentially a
full 10-gig connection.

00:25:15.490 --> 00:25:19.270
So we found a saturation
point in that network.

00:25:19.270 --> 00:25:20.255
So that's performance.

00:25:20.255 --> 00:25:21.630
Everyone talks
about performance.

00:25:21.630 --> 00:25:23.171
You've got to perform
to play, or you

00:25:23.171 --> 00:25:25.540
can't qualify in this business.

00:25:25.540 --> 00:25:26.970
But that's one of
many things that

00:25:26.970 --> 00:25:28.595
matter when you're
taking an enterprise

00:25:28.595 --> 00:25:29.710
application to Cloud.

00:25:29.710 --> 00:25:30.400
All right?

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:34.050
So deployments--
Cloud is typically

00:25:34.050 --> 00:25:35.850
designed to be
click-and-go, and not

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:37.060
worried about infrastructure.

00:25:37.060 --> 00:25:39.690
So this shows you here
what a typical deployment

00:25:39.690 --> 00:25:41.310
looks like with Elastifile.

00:25:41.310 --> 00:25:43.664
You click on the button,
select your project,

00:25:43.664 --> 00:25:46.080
tell it what network you want
to write on, and hit Deploy.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:46.580
OK?

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.655
You're still using one
finger so far, essentially.

00:25:49.655 --> 00:25:51.030
And then it'll
start rolling out.

00:25:51.030 --> 00:25:52.655
If you've ever used
Deployment Manager,

00:25:52.655 --> 00:25:54.330
it's very typical here.

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:57.090
All the components spin up.

00:25:57.090 --> 00:25:58.860
On the far side or
over here you'll

00:25:58.860 --> 00:26:01.140
see it gives you
unique credentials.

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:02.940
You can use a login
for the first time.

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:06.060
This is our console or GUI.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:09.930
All of these functions, again,
are available through our API.

00:26:09.930 --> 00:26:12.120
You tell it what kind of
stores you want to use--

00:26:12.120 --> 00:26:14.795
local SSD, persistent SSD.

00:26:14.795 --> 00:26:17.170
We've actually just added a
few more options to that list

00:26:17.170 --> 00:26:19.420
just in the last
couple of weeks.

00:26:19.420 --> 00:26:23.280
And in no time, once you
hit go this will spin up,

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:25.950
puts a few VMs out there
inside of your project,

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:30.450
and then those services are now
exposed and available to all

00:26:30.450 --> 00:26:33.240
of the workloads you
have running in Cloud.

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:34.020
OK?

00:26:34.020 --> 00:26:34.840
That's it.

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:38.020
That takes about five
minutes, and it's all done.

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:40.760
In fact, if you're into
automation, infrastructure

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:43.560
as code, and things like
Terraform or Ansible

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:47.580
and Puppet, we have options
there for you, as well.

00:26:47.580 --> 00:26:52.490
We've got a Terraform,
essentially a cookbook, out

00:26:52.490 --> 00:26:53.490
on GitHub.

00:26:53.490 --> 00:26:55.980
You can go follow that link
to grab it or grab a solution

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:58.890
article out of our
website on how to do this.

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:01.450
In fact, I'll show you in
less than 60 seconds here.

00:27:01.450 --> 00:27:07.100
We'll let this demo go, assuming
this one will play for us.

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:08.790
And that one is not playing.

00:27:08.790 --> 00:27:12.945
Let's see if we can get it go.

00:27:12.945 --> 00:27:13.940
All right.

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:17.035
It is so fast, you
can't even see it.

00:27:17.035 --> 00:27:18.410
What you would
have seen is a lot

00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:20.192
of code flying
through the screen.

00:27:20.192 --> 00:27:21.650
You give it a
cookbook, you tell it

00:27:21.650 --> 00:27:23.662
what your parameters
are, and deploys it.

00:27:23.662 --> 00:27:26.120
And when you're done, it goes
and destroys it and cleans up

00:27:26.120 --> 00:27:27.755
all of its mess from before.

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:31.760
Next, the storage provisioner.

00:27:31.760 --> 00:27:32.940
This is the one
we're excited about.

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:33.856
We just launched this.

00:27:33.856 --> 00:27:36.950
If you have Kubernetes, which
is containers that are designed

00:27:36.950 --> 00:27:39.260
to be totally
stateless, but you find

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:41.700
that old applications-- not
old, but traditional apps

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:44.270
or in databases and the like--
you want to run as containers

00:27:44.270 --> 00:27:46.970
and get the benefit
of compute, they

00:27:46.970 --> 00:27:49.070
have a hard time
dealing with state.

00:27:49.070 --> 00:27:51.900
You can store data
to a local machine,

00:27:51.900 --> 00:27:54.470
but it's challenging when
you get into tens or hundreds

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:56.330
or thousands of
containers, and they need

00:27:56.330 --> 00:27:57.890
to work off a common dataset.

00:27:57.890 --> 00:28:01.790
This is what we're designed for
here, using standard NFS, which

00:28:01.790 --> 00:28:05.330
comes out of the box working
with a container platform.

00:28:05.330 --> 00:28:08.870
And this allows you to connect
it right into Kubernetes,

00:28:08.870 --> 00:28:11.330
add a storage class.

00:28:11.330 --> 00:28:14.324
It will show up as
Elastifile inside of GKE.

00:28:14.324 --> 00:28:15.740
And now it's a
resource, where you

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:20.360
can do provisioning right to
us, straight from kubectl.

00:28:23.072 --> 00:28:25.280
So there's a concept inside
of Elastifile called data

00:28:25.280 --> 00:28:25.820
containers.

00:28:25.820 --> 00:28:28.430
So a data container
is to files and data

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:31.570
what a compute container
is to your applications.

00:28:31.570 --> 00:28:33.350
It's a bucket, or it
contains everything

00:28:33.350 --> 00:28:35.180
as a file system or
a single namespace

00:28:35.180 --> 00:28:37.760
that you can then manage
as a single entity.

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:42.890
So a container is where we
apply policies to your data,

00:28:42.890 --> 00:28:45.770
underneath-the-cover things like
how much storage does it need?

00:28:45.770 --> 00:28:49.740
We have services like dedupe
and compression built into that.

00:28:49.740 --> 00:28:52.820
And so this demo will show the
creation of a data container.

00:28:52.820 --> 00:28:54.990
Log into the console.

00:28:54.990 --> 00:28:57.380
There's your dashboard, and
all the fancy storage stuff--

00:28:57.380 --> 00:28:59.567
capacities, performance.

00:28:59.567 --> 00:29:02.150
We hit the data container screen
and you'll see a bunch of DCs

00:29:02.150 --> 00:29:04.030
in here.

00:29:04.030 --> 00:29:07.844
Point and click, give it a name.

00:29:07.844 --> 00:29:10.010
We'll be using this one in
the next couple of demos.

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:11.264
We'll turn on.

00:29:11.264 --> 00:29:11.930
Turn on and off.

00:29:11.930 --> 00:29:13.740
You can toggle dedupe and
compression on anytime

00:29:13.740 --> 00:29:14.239
you like.

00:29:14.239 --> 00:29:15.890
And then we set a size.

00:29:15.890 --> 00:29:17.670
This is a dynamic size.

00:29:17.670 --> 00:29:19.080
But this is what will show up.

00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.930
The file system expects
to see a size when

00:29:21.930 --> 00:29:25.230
you connect to your machine.

00:29:25.230 --> 00:29:26.700
So that's it.

00:29:26.700 --> 00:29:28.860
It's created.

00:29:28.860 --> 00:29:31.709
Get some syntax to connect
to it at the standard mount.

00:29:31.709 --> 00:29:33.500
Again, there's no
client-side stuff needed.

00:29:33.500 --> 00:29:36.360
Standard NFS out of a VM works.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.060
So then that's
what it looks like.

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.130
Behind the scenes,
you can see the layers

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.990
of policies of different
features we can add in--

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:48.450
replication,
snapshots, NFS exports.

00:29:48.450 --> 00:29:50.400
And other features
we'll continually

00:29:50.400 --> 00:29:53.630
add with our engineering
team out in Israel

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.100
will start to show up into
here as we continue down

00:29:56.100 --> 00:29:57.599
our journey in Cloud.

00:29:57.599 --> 00:29:59.640
You'll see down on the
bottom, I have the ability

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:00.760
to do user mappings.

00:30:00.760 --> 00:30:02.160
This is NFS language here.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:05.290
If you're used to NFS, this
is very familiar to you.

00:30:05.290 --> 00:30:08.520
So here we're going
to export this share,

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.950
make it available to
just the machines inside

00:30:10.950 --> 00:30:12.450
of my private network.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:13.630
And now ready to go.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:15.750
That's now being
exposed and advertised

00:30:15.750 --> 00:30:19.140
and ready for consumption.

00:30:19.140 --> 00:30:20.820
So let's see it at work.

00:30:20.820 --> 00:30:23.819
I've got a machine, Linux host.

00:30:23.819 --> 00:30:25.610
Again, the message I'm
trying to drive home

00:30:25.610 --> 00:30:28.240
is, this should be normal.

00:30:28.240 --> 00:30:32.030
I do a show mount, and I can see
the exported file systems off

00:30:32.030 --> 00:30:33.500
of that machine.

00:30:33.500 --> 00:30:37.210
I'm going to make a
directory and mount one.

00:30:37.210 --> 00:30:39.000
Got it.

00:30:39.000 --> 00:30:39.780
Move into it.

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:41.130
There's nothing in there.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:44.930
Let me copy some data in.

00:30:44.930 --> 00:30:47.060
So standard commands,
copying data over.

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.030
These things can be more
difficult with Cloud Storage,

00:30:52.030 --> 00:30:53.700
very easy with NFS.

00:30:53.700 --> 00:30:54.200
All right.

00:30:54.200 --> 00:30:57.160
So I'm going to look at a file.

00:30:57.160 --> 00:30:59.130
So I'm showing file
ownership, ACLs.

00:30:59.130 --> 00:31:00.870
This is POSIX information.

00:31:00.870 --> 00:31:04.340
File ownership, all
of the attributes,

00:31:04.340 --> 00:31:05.840
we maintain all of that intact--

00:31:05.840 --> 00:31:09.380
mtime, atime, ctime,
ACLs, everything works.

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:11.870
We just changed it.

00:31:11.870 --> 00:31:13.090
The integrity is there.

00:31:13.090 --> 00:31:14.600
It just works.

00:31:14.600 --> 00:31:15.560
Look familiar?

00:31:15.560 --> 00:31:16.310
Bingo.

00:31:16.310 --> 00:31:18.230
Next topic-- what
about resizing?

00:31:18.230 --> 00:31:20.390
What if I need to change that?

00:31:20.390 --> 00:31:22.910
Coming out of storage world,
if you've done work in SAN,

00:31:22.910 --> 00:31:25.610
this is always a big problem,
any block-based systems.

00:31:25.610 --> 00:31:28.820
The nice thing about NFS is
you can change your mind really

00:31:28.820 --> 00:31:31.860
quickly without really
any sort of an impact.

00:31:31.860 --> 00:31:33.880
So to make the change,
either go into the GUI

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:36.410
or throw a command
through the API.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:37.340
Give it a new size.

00:31:37.340 --> 00:31:41.500
We go from 10 gigabytes
here to 2,000 gigabytes.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:43.930
Update it.

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:46.790
And that's already been
propagated to the VM.

00:31:46.790 --> 00:31:47.697
We'll do a DF again.

00:31:47.697 --> 00:31:50.030
You'll see right on the bottom
we went from 10 gigabytes

00:31:50.030 --> 00:31:52.760
here to 2 terabytes available.

00:31:52.760 --> 00:31:55.070
Easy answer to "I'm
running out of capacity"

00:31:55.070 --> 00:31:57.090
as a problem for the end user.

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:01.430
In fact, we can vastly
over-provision the system.

00:32:01.430 --> 00:32:03.710
We'll just do this
one to be cute.

00:32:03.710 --> 00:32:07.550
We'll go from 2 terabytes
to 20 terabytes.

00:32:07.550 --> 00:32:10.105
We can move into petabyte
range if we wanted to.

00:32:10.105 --> 00:32:12.230
So kind of create your
destiny the way you want it.

00:32:12.230 --> 00:32:13.940
And then let you can
let your capacity--

00:32:13.940 --> 00:32:16.940
the actual allocation doesn't
have to be made until you start

00:32:16.940 --> 00:32:18.420
running out of capacity.

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:20.630
So I could have a
3-terabyte cluster

00:32:20.630 --> 00:32:24.230
that grows into those sizes.

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:28.610
Snapshots-- snapshots are
amazing if you use them

00:32:28.610 --> 00:32:31.160
in development
process for whatever

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:34.670
reasons-- restoring files
or keeping multiple versions

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:36.322
of a database or something.

00:32:36.322 --> 00:32:38.030
We built that right
into the system here.

00:32:38.030 --> 00:32:40.190
So I'll show you a manual
way to take a snapshot.

00:32:40.190 --> 00:32:41.600
And again, same caveat--

00:32:41.600 --> 00:32:44.030
this can all be automated
through API calls

00:32:44.030 --> 00:32:45.470
or other command line functions.

00:32:48.820 --> 00:32:49.990
So snapshot's in place.

